WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy- and Grammy-nominated

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.971
actress and singer you know from
her work on "Glee,"

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.874
"Scream Queens," and
Broadway's "Spring Awakening."

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:11.411
She stars as Fanny Brice
in the Broadway revival

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.079
of "Funny Girl,"
which is playing now

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:14.547
at the August Wilson Theatre.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:17.282
Please welcome to the show
Lea Michele, everyone!

00:00:17.282 --> 00:00:19.419
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.588
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:28.561
♪♪

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:34.567
♪♪

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.970
Hello.
-Oh, my God, Fred Armisen!

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.371
I'm, like, freaking out
right now.

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.908
-I know. It's pretty --
He's hosting the car Oscars.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:45.211
-I heard.
I DVR'd it while I was waiting.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.579
-Oh, yeah, get ready.
You don't want to miss it.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.315
-I'm so excited.
-I'm excited to have you here,

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.785
and I also want to say
how grateful I am

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:54.387
that you are using
your one day off

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.589
in the Broadway schedule week
to be with us.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:57.823
Thank you so much for that.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.892
Oh, no, I'm very happy.
I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:03.396
I know, I do seven shows a week,
and we get Mondays off.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.865
And, yeah, it's
a pretty crazy schedule.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.132
-It is a crazy schedule.

00:01:07.132 --> 00:01:09.235
I know you have
a 2 1/2-year-old.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.304
-Is there at least
some upside to the fact

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.672
that you get to spend
your mornings with a child?

00:01:13.672 --> 00:01:15.742
-Oh, it's great.
I thought about it at first.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.243
I was like, "Okay,
this is gonna work out.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.978
I have the mornings
with the baby,

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.646
pretty much the whole day,
because I don't get to

00:01:20.646 --> 00:01:23.650
the theater till 7:30
for our half-hour.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.619
But it's sort of like going from
one full job,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.689
then to another full job.
I have two kids.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.823
-Oh, I didn't realize
you have two.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:32.492
-No, I don't.
The show is the second.

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.527
-The show is the second.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:38.198
But I know what you mean.
I think, when you get to the end

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:40.232
of a full day with children,
the last thing you want to do

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:42.735
is a Broadway show.
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:46.039
If I didn't love it
so, so, so much,

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.973
then, you know,
it would be much harder.

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:50.643
-Well, fans of "Glee"
know, obviously,

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.512
you've sung all these songs
before.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:55.248
-Yeah.
-This is a very important show

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:57.117
to you personally.
What's it like --

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:59.419
Did it feel like
a culmination of things

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.387
to finally be playing
Fanny Brice onstage?

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.890
-It's wild. It's really --
This, you know, show

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.093
has been following me throughout
the course of my life.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.595
I first watched the movie
when I was in "Spring Awakening"

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.164
on Broadway.
And then I did so much of it

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:15.434
on "Glee."
And now I'm a mom and a wife,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.069
and I'm doing it,
and I really feel

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:19.305
like this is the perfect time
in my life

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.809
to be doing this show.
And it's so different, too.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.044
Like, when we did it on "Glee,"
we were performing it

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:26.746
in a studio, which was fun.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:29.749
But now, like, getting to do it
on a stage with the audience

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.885
and getting to just have that,
it's so incredible.

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:35.087
It's such a dream come true.
-And one of the things

00:02:35.087 --> 00:02:38.658
about this role, obviously, is,
Barbra Streisand played it,

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.027
originated it.
It does seem as though

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.028
a blessing from her
would be important

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:44.363
to someone like you
taking it on.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.933
And you have officially heard
from Barbra Streisand.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:49.469
-Oh, my gosh. I did.
I finally heard from Barbra.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.570
I got to work the other day,

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.507
and my dresser, Alyssa,
who I love so much,

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.909
had this, like, gold envelope.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:57.977
It was like -- I don't know --

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.311
"Charlie and
the Chocolate Factory,"

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:01.981
like, getting invited.
I would think it would arrive

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.249
on, like, doves,
like a special carrier.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:05.918
-Heavy?
Was there a weight to it?

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:08.121
-No, it was light as a feather.
-Oh, right.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:09.188
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.257
And it just --
And she was there,

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:12.525
and she was like,
"I have this for you."

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.894
And I just knew, and I was like,
"Is it from Barbra?"

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:17.163
And she said it was.
And it was very sweet,

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.597
and she wrote me
this beautiful letter.

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:21.067
But one thing she said in it
that I just was so --

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.269
She was like,
"It's really wonderful

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:24.770
when your dreams come true,
isn't it?"

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.940
And I just fell to my knees and
I called Ryan Murphy, my mom,

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.808
Jonathan Groff,
and Michael Mayer.

00:03:29.808 --> 00:03:31.977
Like, these are the people
who need to know.

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:34.247
But it was great.
And then I got a little nervous

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.548
because I was like,
"Wait a second.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.550
How do I not know
that this is not fake?"

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.418
-Yeah.
-That some person is just,

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:43.323
you know, playing the worst
prank on me in the entire world.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.758
-You would have to be
a sociopath to write

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:48.494
that "dreams come true" line
as a prank.

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:50.096
The prank is really --
-No, but it did.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.764
For a second there,
I was like, "Wait a minute."

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:53.899
And then I knew
that my co-star, Ramin,

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:56.302
had gotten a letter from her
when the show first opened,

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:58.470
because he knows her.
He's performed with her before.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.674
So I snuck into his room,
found his copy of his letter,

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:05.411
and then just made sure
that the writing was the same.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.214
And it was.
So that was all I needed.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.848
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:11.384
He was like,
"Were you in my room?"

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.319
I was like, "Yes." [ Laughs ]

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.054
-You were throwing things
out of drawers?

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.123
-Everything was
just completely...

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.624
-He's like,
"Either I've been robbed

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.626
or Lea Michele came in here
to verify handwriting."

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.662
-Exactly. That's what it was.
I don't know why

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.163
that wasn't the first thing
that he thought of.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:28.768
-You -- I think that the stress
of taking on a show like this,

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:31.003
even though you know
you can sing the songs,

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:33.639
is exponentially worse

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:36.008
because you had to teach
yourself how to tap.

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:38.377
-Luckily, I didn't teach myself.
They had people.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:40.379
-Yeah, that's true.
-Could you imagine?

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:42.314
I was like, "Lea, here we go."
-You watched YouTube.

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:44.016
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.785
They were like, "This is
a really important part.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:49.721
We really want to make sure
that you can do this,"

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:52.191
because it is
such a special part in the show

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.160
that, you know, I get to do
so much as Fanny.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.229
And then,
close to the end of Act 2,

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:58.364
it's like, "And now
we're gonna tap for you."

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:01.233
And I just went in,
like, super confident.

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:03.269
I was like, "I got this."

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.471
You know, like,
"I'm just going to totally

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:06.839
learn how to do this,
like nothing.

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:08.374
I've never tapped in my life."

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:12.044
I'm like, "I've had a kid.
I birthed a child, I can tap."

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:15.047
And then I went in,
and within like five minutes,

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:17.683
I think our amazing
tap choreographer

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:21.086
saw the blood just, like,
drain from my face,

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:24.423
and I just, like, knew it.
I was like, "I can't do this."

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:27.193
And I had to like go into
the mirror in the bathroom

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:28.827
and have a conversation
with myself.

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:31.263
But I learned it,
I figured it out,

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:33.032
and now I get to tap on Broadway
every night.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.167
-That's great. I love it.
-Which is amazing, yeah.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.269
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:38.871
-I bet everybody
has that moment.

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:40.773
I think they're like,
"If you want to --

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.842
We have a talking-to-yourself
mirror in the back."

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:45.678
-Oh, yeah, I do it a lot.
I do it a lot. [ Laughs ]

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:48.647
-22 costume changes in the show.
-Yeah.

00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:50.583
-That is --
-Seven --

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.218
There's like five or six wigs,

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:55.254
something like
seven wig changes,

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:57.623
And yeah.
-I know you're working

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.359
with the absolute best
professionals in the world

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:02.127
when you're doing
those kind of quick changes.

00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:03.963
-Yeah.
-Any close calls, so far?

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:06.031
-Some close calls.
Some close calls.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:09.569
I do one change that's behind
a scrim on the stage,

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:13.806
and one of our amazing actresses
in the show is there with me,

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:15.775
and she's helping me to change,
Effie.

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.811
And the scrim just quickly
teetered really quick.

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.113
We, like, looked at each other,
and I was like, "Wouldn't that

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:23.882
just be great if it
just completely fell down?

00:06:23.882 --> 00:06:26.953
Ramin is there singing.
I'm there half naked."

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:29.922
But I'm like, "You know what?
If any show, this is, like,

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:31.891
the perfect show where Fanny
would think of some, like,

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:34.327
hilarious line to say,
and everyone would laugh.

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:36.595
And we would make it --
we'd make it work.

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:39.165
-I think everyone who was there
would not complain.

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:40.599
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:43.202
What about, have you --
You know, a show like this,

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.938
obviously, you're gonna
have people who show up

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:48.307
that you met over the course
of your career

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:50.376
or maybe people who you've seen
over the course of their career.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:52.945
Have you seen any
recognizable faces in the crowd?

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:54.212
-It's funny, though,
'cause everyone --

00:06:54.212 --> 00:06:55.948
some people that I know
that come to the show,

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:58.117
they're like, "Do you want to
know like if I'm there?"

00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:00.419
Or they'll be like, I didn't
want to tell you I was there."

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:01.821
I'm like, "No, I love it."

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:06.725
I love, you know, Ryan Murphy,
who's my dear friend --

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:08.294
he's come to see the show
now three times.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.363
And every time he comes, I,
like, give him a little thing.

00:07:11.363 --> 00:07:12.564
I think it's great.

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:14.700
But Nicole Kidman was there
the other night,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.368
and I saw her,
and I, like, froze.

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:18.704
And I was like --
'Cause no one's wearing masks,

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.506
and you can see everyone now.

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.175
So I got nervous
for, like, a second,

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:23.609
and then I was like, "Okay."

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:26.245
But it's so great
'cause it's super interactive.

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.214
And coming from having --
my last Broadway show,

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.315
"Spring Awakening," was

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:31.951
very serious, intense
subject matter.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.753
But this is, like, we get to
have so much fun every night.

00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:37.289
It's something new
and different and fun.

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:39.592
-I am also so excited --

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:40.926
and I think everybody else
here, as well --

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:42.261
that you're going to do
a song for us tonight.

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:44.863
-I am! [ Laughs ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:44.863 --> 00:07:48.167
-Also the cast album
is available for --

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:51.170
-Yes, the cast album!
-So, I can't believe

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.072
I'm gonna send you off
to get ready for a Broadway show

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:54.439
even though
you have the night off.

00:07:54.439 --> 00:07:55.875
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:58.710
You guys, that's Lea Michele.
"Funny Girl" is playing now

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.012
at the August Wilson Theatre
in New York.

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:01.347 line:0%
We'll be right back
with Janelle James.

